In this essay, I will be demonstrating why ‘race’ is understood as a social construction
rather than a biological essence. The fundamental thesis of the essay is that ‘race’ is a
“hegemonic manifestation” over time, place and history that results from complex inner
relationships between social-macrodynamics and social-microdynamics. As a result, I
argue that ‘race’ is a simulacrum with no reference to anything real (i.e., biological), but
yields significant power as a hyperreal social phenomenon. It “becomes real” via its social
construction.

The genealogical linage of the concept of ‘race’ is where our macro analysis begins. The
process of colonialism was underpinned by a psychological aspect of dehumanising the
colonised (Jamshed, 2023). European colonialisation relied on the hegemonic
classification and dehumanisation of humans based upon arbitrary physical
characteristics, known as ‘race,’ as its justification (Smedley, 2018). Emerging from this
are the binary opposites “civilised” (white, European) and “savage” (colonised indigenous
populations), resulting in the “civilising mission” of colonialism (Petitjean, 2005). In this
sense, the emergence of ‘race’ as understood today is a colonial project used to
dehumanise the “savage” and heroise the “civilised,” emerging as a hegemonic
manifestation used to justify the means and ends of colonial empires (Steinmetz, 2015). In
a Foucauldian power-knowledge sense (Foucault, 1976), the power of ‘race’ is constituted
through its various accepted forms of knowledge and “truth” within a colonially emergent
épistémè (Taylor, 2011). The European colonial powers classify, categorise, and
dehumanise people based on physical characteristics, this “knowledge” was then used to
dominate and brutalise colonised populations, justifying it in the name of “civilising.” In this
sense, then, ‘race’ becomes a biopower, the power to “make live and let die” (Foucault,
1976, p. 241). This is to say, the power to manage the life and death of humans based
upon these social categories. The purpose of constructing ‘race’, therefore, is to dominate,
discipline and regulate people and their populations based upon a hierarchical
categorisation justified by their supposed biological “truth”. ‘Race,’ thus, becomes an
omnipresent methodology of life and death, which thereby reinforces a racial power
structure (Taylor, 2011).

