Another major reason ‘race’ is understood as social, is due to the way racial epistemology
functions, particularly in its duality. Henceforth, we will be engaging with critical poststructural analysis, specifically a synthesis of Deleuze and Guattari with Derrida. Derrida’s
concept of différance states that meaning is unfixed and constantly deferred and
differentiated (Derrida, 1982). Fundamentally, it says that since words and signs are only
comprehensible via their difference from and to other words and signs, then meaning itself
is eternally deferred (Derrida, 1982). This process is endless because words and signs will
always rely on a source external to them for comprehension and thus, are deferred to such
ends forever. This concept, thereby, significantly damages the biological account of race,
and presents race as a fundamentally fluid social concept whose meaning is constantly
constructed through differences never complete or fully knowable. In A Thousand
Plateaus, Deleuze and Guattari describe the rhizome as a system of thinking
characterised by an absence of linearity, hierarchy, and centralisation (Deleuze & Guattari,
1980). It is contrasted with traditional, arborescent “tree-like” structures of thought that has
concrete branches that rely on linearity, hierarchy and centralisation necessarily implying
singular origins of knowledge. Thus, there is a split in the epistemological framework of
‘race’, the colonial/biological form of racial epistemology and the social constructionist
form. Colonial epistemology results from the colonial imposition of a racial binary opposite
(and thus race itself) and is an arborescent structure which attempts to categorise people
based on a centralised reductive hierarchy. Centralised in its early form in scientific racism,
which was also seen as the singular point of origin for arborescent-colonial epistemology
and knowledge of 'race'. On the other hand, the actual form of racial epistemology – the
social form, is rhizomatic in nature. It is not based on any kind of hierarchy, knowledge
linearity or centralised epistemic authority. What colonial epistemology has therefore relied
on is the deflection and control of how words/signs pertaining to 'race' are deferred via the
process of différance within the context of its colonial episteme. In other words, instead of
racial deferrals pointing to things that are suggestive of social construction, colonial
epistemology sees to it that the deferrals of race point to things that suggest a biological
reality to race. Phenotypical essentialism is a perfect example of this deferral deflection
and control. Phrenology has historically relied on the linking between physical
characteristics such as skin colour and skull shape with so called inherent behavioural,
intellectual or cultural traits. As a deflection, this controlling function of deferral allows a
false link between biology and "predetermined behavioural traits", thereby resulting in
deferrals towards ideas that fundamentally support biological essentialism rather than
social constructionism. This allows the resultant creation of illusory fixed, “biological”
