identities resulting from the non-rhizomatic traditionalist forms of epistemology that colonial
power structures have relied on.

Now, the immensely complex result of everything discussed hitherto is that ‘race’ has
achieved “hyperreality”, it is a “simulacrum” without reference to anything real, biological or
concrete. According to Baudrillard, simulacra are representations of things that don’t have
an authentic reference point (Baudrillard, 1985). For Baudrillard, a simulacrum replaces
reality with simulation, and at some point, becomes more true than reality itself, despite
having no reference to reality. ‘Race’ became a socially created simulacrum by evolving
from being a reflection of coloniality and power, to a distorted visage of arbitrary biological
differences, to a hegemonically ingrained discourse at the macro and micro levels, and
finally to a pure simulacrum with no reference to anything real in the biological sense.
What this process has created is a form of hyperreality, in which the reality and unreality of
whatever ‘race’ is, has become entirely distorted, with no clear indication of where fiction
starts, and reality begins. This is most evident in how discourse around race in the media
is constantly underpinned by implied or explicit biological race essentialism, despite there
being no apparent reason to think ‘race’ is an ontologically real category (Hughey & Goss,
2015). This hyperreality means that the “signs” of race have become more important in
discourse than the actual reality behind human biology and diversity.

Now we must determine what effect ‘race’ achieving hyperreality has on the micro-level
and how this helps us understand why race is seen not as a biological construct but as a
social one. Here, we will engage in Husserl-Wittgenstein analysis. Edmund Husserl
developed the concepts of the lifeworld and intersubjectivity. The lifeworld is the totality of
ones lived social everyday reality and consists within it how we’ve come to understand and
subjectively perceive the world (Husserl, 1970). It is not an ontologically external reality,
outside of us, but is instead our own constructed reality. Intersubjectivity is thus the mutual
co-understanding of reality that emerges from interactions with others (Husserl, 1970), and
since such interactions are necessarily social, intersubjectivity has profound implications
for micro-sociological hegemony. Applying this to race, what we might term hegemonic
intersubjectivity arises from racially motivated interactions, whereupon a "hegemonic
consensus” arises from processes of racialisation at the micro level. From
microaggressions to racial profiling, racialised interactions uphold the hegemonic
4

consensus formed from intersubjectivity by reinforcing the concept of 'race' as a reliable
indication of judgement and racial stereotypes themselves. In Philosophical Investigations,
Wittgenstein introduces the concept of language games (Wittgenstein, 1953). To
Wittgenstein, a language game is a particular social context in which words within that
context take on meanings that are only attributable to said context. In a sense, the idea of
language games is that words and meaning are foundationally contextual and social. The
lifeworld takes on the hegemonic dye of the intersubjectivity, and thus a hegemonic
manifestation ingrained in language games becomes dominant throughout the interactive
level of the social world. ‘Race’, in this sense, comes to be seen as “biological” through a
hegemonic interactive consensus that makes up the lifeworld. Thus, ‘race’ as a concept is
not merely reproduced at a macro-level, but is in fact dependent on being reproduced at
the interactive level, expanding from language games, into intersubjectivity and finally at
the individual level of the lifeworld. Let us take the model minority stereotype as a case
study. In many school settings, there is a “hegemonic consensus” that “students of race X
are academically superior”, which is reproduced through hegemonic interactions and then
expanded across shared intersubjectivity and language games (Lindsley-Kim, 2023). The
consequence of this, is that ‘race’ has become ingrained into everyday thinking, which
reinforces its supposed biological and essentialist basis in the backdrop of race having
achieved hyperreality. The great irony in this process is that ‘race’, a social construct, is
socially defined, reproduced and ingrained, leading to a point in which it is seen as
genuinely biological - real. ‘Race’, via its social construction and reproduced
hegemonically at every level of society, becomes “biological”.

The final task of this essay is to understand precisely how ‘race’ becomes real via its own
social construction, how it being a social category is what makes it so remarkably easy for
people to see as a real category, as a biological reality. For this, we will use Schutz-Sacks
analysis. Within his social phenomenology, Alfred Schutz developed the concept of
typification, the process by which people are categorised based upon common
characteristics which have differing presumed meanings based upon situational context,
which we then use to try and interact more clearly with the world (Schutz, 1962 & 1967).
Typification necessitates the simplification of human complexity and diversity via the
creation of concrete generalised types that can be easily recognised and reproduced.
Typification is at the centre of the social construction of reality, particularly because as a
process it is purely dependent upon the intersubjectivity and lifeworld of both the typifier
5

and the typified. Social typification allows individuals to make hasty social judgements
based upon readily observable characteristics, including ‘race’. Influenced by Schutz,
Harvey Sacks introduced the concept of Membership Categorisation Device (MCD). MCDs
are specific collections or sets of categories and social rules assigned to interactions
(Sacks, 1995). For instance, the MCD “family” includes inside it categories such as
“mother”, “sister”, “aunt”, etc and each of these categories have implied or explicit rules
within them pertaining to how they relate to each other. Applying these to race, we see a
profound display of social construction and reality-making on display. Throughout historical
processes of colonialism, imperialism, slavery, social hierarchy and stratification based on
physical features, a racial MCD has emerged with categories such as “White”, “Black”,
“Asian” etc etc. Using the categories within the colonially emergent racial MCD, individuals
typify others relying upon implied stereotypes of these categories for social simplification.
The constant typification of categories based on the overarching membership
categorisation device results in the reification of race, where it goes from being an abstract
psychological aspect of coloniality to being perceived as a biological reality rather than a
social construct despite that very social construction being the reason ‘race’ is perceived
as real. If reality is necessarily socially constructed in the phenomenological sense,
concepts "become real" via their very own social constructionism; ‘race’ is perhaps the
most consequential of these constructions. ‘Race’ has no biological basis but becomes
“real” through its social construction.

In conclusion, we have analysed why race is understood as a social construct via
genealogical-Foucauldian analysis of coloniality, and how 'race' has become a biopower.
We have also come to understand how colonial epistemology functions, in particular how it
deflects racial deferrals into implied racial essentialism. We have understood how 'race'
became "hyperreal" and how this effects the micro level via Husserl-Wittgenstein analysis,
emphasising the relationship between hegemony and intersubjectivity and finally we have
understood via Schutz-Sacks analysis how 'race' becomes "real" via the typification of
colonially emergent MCDs.

